<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Changping</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">262–260 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Changping, fought from 262 to 260 BCE near present-day Gaoping in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, was the decisive clash between the Chinese states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and Zhao. The Qin general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bai-qi">Bai Qi</a> surrounded the Zhao army under Zhao Kuo, who was killed. Ancient sources claim that some 400,000 surrendered Zhao soldiers were then massacred, probably an exaggeration, but Zhao never recovered its strength.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-changping.jpg" alt="Battle of Changping" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Changping.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 265 BCE Qin attacked the State of Han and took Qinyang, cutting off Han's Shangdang Commandery from its southern heartland. Rather than let it fall to Qin, its governor, Feng Ting, offered the region to Zhao. Shangdang lay just west of the Zhao capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handan">Handan</a>, so King Xiaocheng of Zhao accepted and sent the senior general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lian-po">Lian Po</a> to hold it. In 262 BCE a Qin army under Wang He invaded Shangdang, and Lian Po built three defensive lines south of the Changping Pass.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After early defeats lost him the first line, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lian-po">Lian Po</a> avoided battle in the open and held a fortified line along the Dan River, hoping to exhaust an enemy fighting far from home. By July the two sides faced each other across the river, and the stalemate lasted two years, with around a million combatants by early 260 BCE. Qin spies then spread rumours that Lian Po was old and cowardly, and the king replaced him with Zhao Kuo, son of the late general Zhao She, who had never fought a battle. Qin secretly gave its command to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bai-qi">Bai Qi</a>.</p><p>In July 260 BCE Zhao Kuo crossed the river to attack. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> left fell back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a>, drawing him on, while a detachment of 25,000 men swung behind the Zhao lines and 5,000 cavalry cut his army off from its supply depot. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-zhaoxiang-of-qin">King Zhaoxiang of Qin</a> called up every able-bodied man over the age of 15 in Henei to tighten the ring. Besieged for 46 days and starving, the Zhao army surrendered after Zhao Kuo was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> in a last attempt to break out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Unable to guard or feed so many prisoners, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bai-qi">Bai Qi</a> had them executed, presumably by burying them alive, and released only 240 of the youngest to spread terror. The Han historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>, writing about 150 years later, is the main source for the death toll. King Zhaoxiang refused Bai Qi's wish to march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handan">Handan</a> at once; Bai Qi later declined command and was forced to kill himself, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> defeat at the Battle of Handan halted its expansion for almost three decades. Zhao never recovered, and Qin unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 221 BCE. Zhao Kuo's failure gave the idiom talking wars on paper.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Changping” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the <b>Battle of Changping</b> in 260 BCE he destroyed the army of Zhao; the traditional claim that 400,000 surrendered men were then killed is probably exaggerated, but mass graves have been found at the site.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>But at <b>Changping</b> in 260 BCE the State of Qin destroyed its main army, reportedly killing hundreds of thousands of prisoners.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-zhao">State of Zhao</a></li></ul></section>
